  "Number": 6,
  "Question": "What non-zero real value for \\(x\\) satisfies \\((7x)^{14} = (14x)^7?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{7}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(14\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Since \\(14x=2(7x)\\), the right side contains a seventh power of \\(7x\\). Use the power-of-a-product rule to write\n\\[\n(7x)^{14}=2^7(7x)^7.\n\\]\nThe condition \\(x\\ne0\\) ensures that \\((7x)^7\\ne0\\), so dividing both sides by it is valid. This gives\n\\[\n(7x)^7=2^7.\n\\]\nThe positive right side forces \\(7x>0\\). Among positive numbers, seventh powers increase with the base, so the bases must be equal: \\(7x=2\\). Hence \\(x=2/7\\).\n\nFor this value, the original equation becomes \\(2^{14}=4^7\\), which holds because \\(4^7=(2^2)^7=2^{14}\\). Thus the unique nonzero real solution is \\(\\boxed{\\frac27}\\), choice B.",
